#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: named-arg-defaults
description: a named argument for a defaulted parameter binds BY NAME, and the remaining parameters come from their defaults
backends: [int, js, jvm]
---

# Named arguments bind by name, not by position

`BUGS.md` `standard-tier-named-arg-skip-default`. Both self-hosted fronts dropped the label and
lowered the value positionally, and they did it *differently*: F emitted a short application
(`ssc: arity: 4 expected, 2 given`, loud) while the legacy front put the value in the **first
omitted slot** (`b=C1 c=C0 d=D0`, silent, exit 0). The filed report also claimed that naming the
FIRST defaulted parameter worked — re-measured 2026-07-28, it did not.

Every line below is a shape the fix has to get right, and each one failed differently before it:
name a middle parameter, name the last, name the first, name two, name two **out of order**, and
the two shapes that always worked (no named args at all, and a fully positional call) as the guard
that the ordinary paths were not disturbed.

Object-method named args (`O.m(1, c = 9)`) were deliberately absent when this case was written: the
v1 interpreter — this suite's `int` golden — bound them to the wrong parameter too, so pinning the
correct answer would have made the case red for the golden's own defect rather than for the
compilers'. **That is fixed** (`v1-interp-object-method-named-arg-wrong-slot`), so the shape is
covered here now, which is what the fix was for — the `dispatch*` signature is positional, so the
method-call path had been dropping the label and letting the value fall into the first defaulted
slot.

Class-method named args are still absent, and for the same reason one lane at a time: `K().m(1,
c = 9)` gives `NaN` on the JS lane, and a default that reads a field (`def shift(dx: Int = x)`)
gave `6/NaN`. That was `js-class-method-named-arg-nan`, and it is **FIXED** — the class-method rows
below were added with the fix, so this case now covers object AND class methods.

Two causes sat behind that one symptom, and either alone still failed:
a class's METHODS were never registered in the param-order table (`deep` walked objects and a case
class's CONSTRUCTOR, never a class BODY), so named arguments were not reordered; and class-method
DEFAULTS were dropped entirely, because the emitted signature was built from bare parameter names.
The `dx: Int = x` row is the sharp one — its default reads a FIELD, which only exists after the
`_self` destructuring, so the defaults have to be applied in the body rather than as JS default
parameters.

```scalascript
def f(a: String, b: String = "B0", c: String = "C0", d: String = "D0"): String =
  "b=" + b + " c=" + c + " d=" + d

case class P(x: Int, y: Int = 2, z: Int = 3)

object O:
  def m(a: Int, b: Int = 5, c: Int = 7): Int = a + b * 10 + c * 100

class K:
  def m(a: Int, b: Int = 5, c: Int = 7): Int = a + b * 10 + c * 100

case class P2(x: Int, y: Int = 2):
  def shift(dx: Int = x, dy: Int = 0): String = (x + dx).toString + "/" + (y + dy).toString

println(f("x", c = "C1"))
println(f("x", d = "D1"))
println(f("x", b = "B1"))
println(f("x", c = "C1", d = "D1"))
println(f("x", d = "D1", b = "B1"))
println(f("x"))
println(f("x", "B2", "C2", "D2"))
println(P(1, z = 9).y)
println(P(1, z = 9).z)
println(P(1).z)
println(O.m(1, c = 9))
println(O.m(1, b = 2))
println(O.m(a = 1, c = 9))
println(O.m(c = 9, a = 1))
println(O.m(1))
println(O.m(1, 2, 3))
println(O.m(1, b = 2, c = 3))

// CLASS methods — the half this case could not cover until the fix.
println(K().m(1, c = 9))
println(K().m(1, b = 2))
println(K().m(c = 9, a = 1))
println(K().m(1))
println(K().m(1, 2, 3))
// a default that reads a FIELD, evaluated after the receiver's fields are in scope
println(P2(5).shift(dy = 1))
println(P2(5).shift(dx = 100))
// NOT `P2(5).shift()` — the no-argument call. jvm and js both answer `10/2`; the INT lane dies
// with `Undefined: x` evaluating the field-valued default, so including it would pin a divergence
// in a case that exists to gate the js fix. Filed as
// `int-field-valued-default-undefined-on-empty-call`.
```
